1
00:00:07,204 --> 00:00:07,964
[SPEAKER_00]: What is choice?

2
00:00:14,489 --> 00:00:19,693
[SPEAKER_00]: I was going to say, yeah, what chooses?

3
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:22,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I tend to this site?

4
00:00:22,895 --> 00:00:24,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is there attention to the sound?

5
00:00:25,097 --> 00:00:26,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's the same.

6
00:00:26,918 --> 00:00:29,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Asking why, it's the same type of question.

7
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:34,624
[SPEAKER_01]: I told you, just in our regard to the senses.

8
00:00:35,298 --> 00:00:41,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Why and who chooses and stuff, all those are assumptions based on the fact that you're confused in regard to choice.

9
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is choice.

10
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:44,921
[SPEAKER_00]: There's choice.

11
00:00:45,281 --> 00:00:46,081
[SPEAKER_00]: There is attention.

12
00:00:46,421 --> 00:00:47,121
[SPEAKER_00]: There's attention.

13
00:00:47,961 --> 00:00:50,802
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is intention in regard to...

14
00:00:51,742 --> 00:00:52,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

15
00:00:52,863 --> 00:00:54,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

16
00:00:54,749 --> 00:01:17,383
[SPEAKER_00]: If I take it, if I'm aware of myself, plus I'm seeing intention, thoughts, thinking about, I'm going to do this, do that, and I'm also seeing what I'm attending to, seeing attention, that's also a valuable practice, to see that.

17
00:01:17,723 --> 00:01:19,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it is, but...

18
00:01:19,924 --> 00:01:21,185
[SPEAKER_00]: It's separate things.

19
00:01:22,146 --> 00:01:22,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

20
00:01:24,577 --> 00:01:30,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Most likely, that which you think yourself is in that setup, won't be really yourself.

21
00:01:30,423 --> 00:01:33,286
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be your idea of what yourself was at the time when you thought of it.

22
00:01:34,667 --> 00:01:37,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Because yourself... that's what we meant before.

23
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:46,619
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are able to choose, will, intend, without inducing sense of I am, through that choice, will or intention,

24
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:48,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you'll be another one.

25
00:01:48,615 --> 00:01:50,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, on the way to be one.

26
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:55,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, again, mindfulness of reading ānāpānasati is taught to sotāpannas.

27
00:01:55,222 --> 00:01:58,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody shorter than that can really understand it and do it properly.

28
00:01:58,906 --> 00:02:07,115
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there is a right view, then bringing that sense of, I am choosing, I am willing, I am doing it like this, I'm doing it like that,

29
00:02:08,087 --> 00:02:13,429
[SPEAKER_01]: makes you include yourself always and not exclude and make it kind of disappear in the background.

30
00:02:13,949 --> 00:02:17,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, that mindfulness and that discernment applies to it.

31
00:02:20,172 --> 00:02:28,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you want to do, you have to... you can't just directly start seeing yourself choosing, because yourself will step outside of that and take it as yours.

32
00:02:29,415 --> 00:02:32,256
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it won't be your sense of self, it will be elsewhere.

33
00:02:32,857 --> 00:02:33,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you want to do is

34
00:02:34,962 --> 00:02:36,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognize the inevitability of the choice.

35
00:02:36,863 --> 00:02:38,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to go the indirect way.

36
00:02:39,365 --> 00:02:42,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Realize that, well, you can but not choose.

37
00:02:43,388 --> 00:02:45,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And whatever you do, that's choice.

38
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:49,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're actually not free in regard to choice.

39
00:02:49,912 --> 00:02:55,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You might be free to choose what you want particularly, but you can't... you're not free to not choose at all.

40
00:02:57,217 --> 00:03:03,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... in that recognition, that's what it's... the kind of... it contains

41
00:03:04,712 --> 00:03:11,935
[SPEAKER_01]: The understanding that even the most volitional act and choice cannot be actually taken as yours.

42
00:03:13,235 --> 00:03:21,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they fully depend upon... well, they're given beforehand, as the actual thing, as the possibilities.

43
00:03:21,978 --> 00:03:25,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't choose something out of nothingness.

44
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:28,401
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only the implicit.

45
00:03:29,881 --> 00:03:31,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's a random spontaneous act,

46
00:03:32,331 --> 00:03:36,853
[SPEAKER_01]: It's implicit as such, and that's why you could have attended it and choose to do it.

47
00:03:38,814 --> 00:03:39,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Chosen to do it.

48
00:03:43,515 --> 00:03:57,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, indirectly, you have to kind of hollow it out, so to speak, until the thing is not being fed anymore through the false assumption, through the wrong assumption of, I am choosing this.

49
00:03:58,161 --> 00:04:00,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, the choice remains, the preferences remain,

50
00:04:02,011 --> 00:04:06,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're not being fed anymore and they don't spread, they don't proliferate through ignorance.

51
00:04:06,973 --> 00:04:08,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like a palm stump.

52
00:04:09,314 --> 00:04:10,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You destroy the roots.

53
00:04:11,095 --> 00:04:20,139
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not modifying choices, you're not trimming the branches, landscaping your tree or whatever else.

54
00:04:20,339 --> 00:04:20,519
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

55
00:04:20,979 --> 00:04:23,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the problem is in the roots.

56
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,321
[SPEAKER_01]: They are feeding this whole proliferation of that tree.

57
00:04:26,842 --> 00:04:27,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you kill the roots.

58
00:04:29,023 --> 00:04:29,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whatever's

59
00:04:30,499 --> 00:04:32,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever way it evolves and grows, it stays.

60
00:04:33,421 --> 00:04:35,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And the point is, it won't go further.

61
00:04:35,343 --> 00:04:37,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how it stayed.

62
00:04:37,985 --> 00:04:40,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why our arms can still have habits and stuff like that.

63
00:04:41,889 --> 00:04:42,869
[SPEAKER_01]: External habits.

64
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:46,813
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of it is being fueled and fed through the roots of greed, aversion, delusion.

65
00:04:48,854 --> 00:04:51,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're fully aware, mindful of your body again,

66
00:04:52,595 --> 00:05:03,397
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're mindful of the body as a thing out there in the world, because of which your whole experience right now exists, and any choice you make, without actually making any specific choice, is then included in that.

67
00:05:04,457 --> 00:05:12,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that, no matter how far your awareness and mindfulness go, it will always remain determined by this living body out there.

68
00:05:12,919 --> 00:05:14,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the body is dead, this cannot stand.

69
00:05:16,439 --> 00:05:19,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And that already includes the nature of the choice within that.

70
00:05:21,172 --> 00:05:22,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to start like that.

71
00:05:22,913 --> 00:05:30,839
[SPEAKER_01]: When that view gets established, then you have to kind of go within that and clear out all the residues inside.

72
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:37,824
[SPEAKER_01]: But first, you have to start from the outside, which is why Sotāpanna still has conceit and so on, that he needs to kind of purify later.

73
00:05:37,844 --> 00:05:42,007
[SPEAKER_01]: But he doesn't have that general, fundamental, wrong view.

74
00:05:44,389 --> 00:05:48,192
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the starting point is that

75
00:05:50,911 --> 00:05:56,696
[SPEAKER_01]: The general experience as a whole is seen determined by the body.

76
00:05:56,816 --> 00:06:08,906
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you recognize, as we spoke before, that that body would not be even discernible as such, if there is no general experience in consciousness in regard to it.

77
00:06:10,307 --> 00:06:14,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that just everything stands upon the body and the body is out there in the world independent.

78
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:17,053
[SPEAKER_01]: That body out there in the world is actually determined.

79
00:06:17,855 --> 00:06:20,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But this mind depends upon that body.

80
00:06:21,296 --> 00:06:23,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, loosely put, obviously.

81
00:06:24,098 --> 00:06:25,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Nāma-rūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāma-rūpa.

82
00:06:25,878 --> 00:06:35,322
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said before, it's that matter out in the world, with the consciousness out there, with that matter, that your experience exists as such.

83
00:06:35,543 --> 00:06:37,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness is not in your head, or anything like that.

84
00:06:37,884 --> 00:06:39,564
[SPEAKER_01]: It's equally external, out there.

85
00:06:42,606 --> 00:06:45,847
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, it's a bit... conscience is out there.

86
00:06:46,973 --> 00:06:48,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Where matter is.

87
00:06:48,455 --> 00:06:48,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

88
00:06:49,336 --> 00:06:55,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you discern consciousness based on where matter is discerned to be.

89
00:06:55,523 --> 00:06:57,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that actual matter is inaccessible.

90
00:06:57,966 --> 00:07:04,974
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said before, when you think of the four elements, well, that's your thought that stands for that which four elements is.

91
00:07:06,335 --> 00:07:07,537
[SPEAKER_01]: If it actually stands for

92
00:07:09,323 --> 00:07:13,025
[SPEAKER_01]: What the nature of the four great elements is, means you're not conceiving them.

93
00:07:13,606 --> 00:07:15,567
[SPEAKER_01]: That thought is the correct thought.

94
00:07:15,687 --> 00:07:16,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you have understood.

95
00:07:17,288 --> 00:07:20,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, they don't find a footing in your experience anymore.

96
00:07:20,851 --> 00:07:24,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say the four great elements have ceased in another end.

97
00:07:25,954 --> 00:07:38,462
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your thought, the presentation, or dhamma, or phenomena, whatever you want to call it, stands for something that's assumed to be matter, in other words, stands wrongly,

98
00:07:38,973 --> 00:07:42,436
[SPEAKER_01]: This represents that which is matter.

99
00:07:43,156 --> 00:07:44,698
[SPEAKER_01]: That means, ah, you have conceived it.

100
00:07:45,118 --> 00:07:46,399
[SPEAKER_01]: You have conceived the matter.

101
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:48,701
[SPEAKER_01]: The matter found Fuji.

102
00:07:49,361 --> 00:07:51,243
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the whole world exists for you.

103
00:07:52,544 --> 00:07:53,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is more of a reality.

104
00:07:55,066 --> 00:07:57,668
[SPEAKER_00]: But knowing that whatever I think is only a thought.

105
00:07:57,788 --> 00:07:58,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

106
00:07:59,029 --> 00:08:00,470
[SPEAKER_00]: And that matter is unthinkable.

107
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:08,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing that what you think is only a thought, and the matter is unthinkable, and knowing that also is a thought, that doesn't stand for that matter.

108
00:08:09,121 --> 00:08:11,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And that matter, that I just said, is also thought.

109
00:08:12,342 --> 00:08:23,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any possibility, and the more and more peripheral, the peripheral thought in regard to your current thought, doesn't stand for that matter anymore.

110
00:08:24,046 --> 00:08:28,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just actual thought, but the whole nature of peripheral thought doesn't represent it either.

111
00:08:29,969 --> 00:08:32,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can say you have fully approached conceivings.

112
00:08:33,511 --> 00:08:35,232
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't conceive it in any shape or form.

113
00:08:36,272 --> 00:08:41,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Far, near, direct, indirect, anyhow.

114
00:08:41,995 --> 00:08:44,016
[SPEAKER_01]: But then saying, well, so it's all in my head.

115
00:08:44,136 --> 00:08:44,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

116
00:08:45,316 --> 00:08:45,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Well thought.

117
00:08:45,977 --> 00:08:47,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that again?

118
00:08:47,497 --> 00:08:48,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, sure.

119
00:08:48,438 --> 00:08:49,899
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, it is all in your head.

120
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:56,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But would your head be there, if you're not discerning it, if you're not thinking of it as my head?

121
00:08:56,782 --> 00:08:57,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't.

122
00:08:58,117 --> 00:09:06,183
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you recognize, well, would I be even able to think any of that, if this body, this material body out there, is not living and breathing?

123
00:09:07,044 --> 00:09:07,464
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

124
00:09:08,405 --> 00:09:11,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, yeah, it's all in your head, for as long as your body is alive.

125
00:09:13,228 --> 00:09:19,373
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is destroyed, the matter is taken away, there is no more head, there is no more mind.

126
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:26,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognize, well, actually, my mind, the experience as a whole, not

127
00:09:28,498 --> 00:09:34,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Imaginary, mental, whatever, doesn't, it doesn't actually, it's not independent.

128
00:09:35,042 --> 00:09:38,484
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's usually what statement, it's all in my head, implies.

129
00:09:38,764 --> 00:09:43,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, me, and everything else, kind of, basically, I'm the most fundamental.

130
00:09:44,247 --> 00:09:53,213
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize, sure, I am mostly fundamental, and then you take that thought, and recognize it as determined by the fact that matter is there, inaccessible.

131
00:09:54,854 --> 00:09:56,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know, you can know that.

132
00:09:57,955 --> 00:10:01,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if something happens to that matter, everything's gone.

133
00:10:01,676 --> 00:10:02,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can know that.

134
00:10:02,516 --> 00:10:03,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that.

135
00:10:04,417 --> 00:10:08,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing that long enough will bring further recognition.

136
00:10:08,358 --> 00:10:14,879
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even if you have a thought control in my head, you don't take that as me and mine.

137
00:10:14,899 --> 00:10:16,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know that's impermanent.

138
00:10:16,579 --> 00:10:17,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, everything is thought.

139
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:18,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

140
00:10:18,820 --> 00:10:24,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thought, the nature of thought, is able to manifest itself because matter is there, present, alive.

141
00:10:25,782 --> 00:10:26,822
[SPEAKER_01]: That gets destroyed.

142
00:10:27,405 --> 00:10:29,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is another thought.

143
00:10:29,947 --> 00:10:33,930
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's another thought in regard to the method that's present alive, while I'm still alive.

144
00:10:33,950 --> 00:10:36,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is another thought, which is another thought, which... And yeah, it's infinite.

145
00:10:36,792 --> 00:10:38,574
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no end, because you can never reach the matter.

146
00:10:39,274 --> 00:10:49,763
[SPEAKER_01]: But through that infinity, if you want to pursue it or not, you can know, okay, I'm able to go in this any infinite direction for as long as that which is matter stays.

147
00:10:51,364 --> 00:10:54,366
[SPEAKER_01]: If that which is matter gets destroyed, the whole infinity is gone.

148
00:10:54,827 --> 00:10:56,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what I meant when I said

149
00:10:59,023 --> 00:10:59,664
[SPEAKER_01]: How will I put it?

150
00:11:01,824 --> 00:11:07,305
[SPEAKER_01]: When eternal, infinite, is not confused with permanent and self.

151
00:11:08,345 --> 00:11:10,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Because infinite, it's like eternity.

152
00:11:10,906 --> 00:11:14,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You can go forever, for as long as the matter stands.

153
00:11:14,787 --> 00:11:16,387
[SPEAKER_01]: The matter is removed, the whole domain is gone.

154
00:11:16,767 --> 00:11:17,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

155
00:11:21,608 --> 00:11:24,269
[SPEAKER_00]: It almost sounds like I'm going to have to convince myself.

156
00:11:25,229 --> 00:11:26,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, you do.

157
00:11:26,982 --> 00:11:32,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it has to be taken on artificially, like a graph, as Nibbāla said, the right view.

158
00:11:33,267 --> 00:11:48,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you do it long enough, because, or if, you took it rightly, and if it corresponds to the way things actually are, then, yeah, it will take the proper route, and then you can see it for yourself as being confirmed.

159
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:54,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But you won't see it naturally, because that would mean you could find your own way out of ignorance.

160
00:11:57,205 --> 00:11:58,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have to take the right conviction.

161
00:11:58,885 --> 00:11:59,606
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right view.

162
00:12:00,966 --> 00:12:09,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But, there is the assumption, in every kutujana, in different degrees, that things will happen naturally.

163
00:12:09,428 --> 00:12:11,708
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice, and then you feel this thing unraveling.

164
00:12:14,029 --> 00:12:15,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, it happened.

165
00:12:15,469 --> 00:12:16,029
[SPEAKER_01]: I got it.

166
00:12:16,209 --> 00:12:17,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's clear.

167
00:12:19,430 --> 00:12:24,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are kind of very general descriptions, but they're usually taken to mean a wrong thing.

168
00:12:24,788 --> 00:12:25,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You want it to happen.

169
00:12:25,828 --> 00:12:27,769
[SPEAKER_01]: You want enlightenment to be found.

170
00:12:27,889 --> 00:12:30,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's all an easy ride afterwards.

171
00:12:30,970 --> 00:12:31,130
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

172
00:12:31,290 --> 00:12:32,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to convince yourself.

173
00:12:33,250 --> 00:12:35,891
[SPEAKER_01]: And, well, it's not real, if I convince myself.

174
00:12:36,291 --> 00:12:41,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what makes you think that your criteria, what's real, what's not real, is correct?

175
00:12:43,533 --> 00:12:44,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Pratyujana thinks it's real.

176
00:12:44,934 --> 00:12:45,634
[SPEAKER_01]: It's his self.

177
00:12:47,147 --> 00:13:03,076
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he might not go so crudely and think, oh, I am, I'm eternal or whatever, but still, for as long as he knows he's put to it in the human, he thinks, this is real, that's not real, means, ah, this is more important, that's less important, means, I don't actually know important as important, unimportant as unimportant.

178
00:13:04,176 --> 00:13:09,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why that was like the Sotāpanna, that verse says, knowing important is important, unimportant is unimportant.

179
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:12,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting that criteria.

180
00:13:13,721 --> 00:13:14,762
[SPEAKER_01]: And you get criteria,

181
00:13:16,108 --> 00:13:20,410
[SPEAKER_01]: If you convince yourself to such an extent, the recognition takes place.

182
00:13:20,951 --> 00:13:26,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there is a form of natural unravelling then, but not before.

183
00:13:32,857 --> 00:13:36,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, as I said, the Buddha would always make such exclamations like,

184
00:13:40,018 --> 00:13:46,403
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done it.

185
00:13:48,184 --> 00:13:49,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody else had any input.

186
00:13:49,806 --> 00:13:52,088
[SPEAKER_01]: No luck, no circumstances.

187
00:13:52,168 --> 00:13:53,709
[SPEAKER_01]: I've made the effort and I got it.

188
00:13:54,530 --> 00:13:57,012
[SPEAKER_01]: I worked through this conviction until I've seen it.

189
