384
00:25:38,171 --> 00:25:39,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It means virtue.

385
00:25:40,013 --> 00:25:40,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue and duty.

386
00:25:43,095 --> 00:25:44,976
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes again with trying to purify...

387
00:25:44,996 --> 00:25:46,838
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no dhamma without virtue and duty.

388
00:25:47,795 --> 00:25:49,475
[SPEAKER_01]: But dhamma is not virtue, do you?

389
00:25:49,835 --> 00:25:51,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe you have to restrain yourself.

390
00:25:51,496 --> 00:25:54,317
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to restrain yourself first, and then you might see it.

391
00:25:54,637 --> 00:25:56,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you see it, you'll see that it's not the restraint.

392
00:26:00,138 --> 00:26:01,698
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not restrained either.

393
00:26:02,939 --> 00:26:06,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can never... if you just restrain, restrain, restrain, you'll always be restraining yourself, right?

394
00:26:06,880 --> 00:26:07,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

395
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:10,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's no point... or is there?

396
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:17,102
[SPEAKER_00]: A point where you restrain so much, where you can never... you're so virtuous, you don't think anything

397
00:26:19,128 --> 00:26:22,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And that won't result in dhamma.

398
00:26:23,090 --> 00:26:23,590
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

399
00:26:23,730 --> 00:26:26,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people think, if I'm just virtuous enough, I'll see the dhamma.

400
00:26:27,011 --> 00:26:27,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily.

401
00:26:29,632 --> 00:26:30,253
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a basis.

402
00:26:30,413 --> 00:26:33,054
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't put anything on that basis, it remains just a basis.

403
00:26:33,454 --> 00:26:35,374
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's your end restraint?

404
00:26:35,414 --> 00:26:36,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a raft.

405
00:26:36,495 --> 00:26:38,376
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you don't use a raft, it doesn't mean anything.

406
00:26:41,237 --> 00:26:42,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I suppose, yeah.

407
00:26:42,097 --> 00:26:44,438
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it can... like, but I think I said that before.

408
00:26:45,125 --> 00:26:49,926
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a fertile field, but if you don't put any seeds, it's just a fertile field.

409
00:26:49,966 --> 00:26:53,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't expect, you know, ripening the fruit.

410
00:26:54,207 --> 00:26:55,867
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an ant, because you have planted it.

411
00:26:56,307 --> 00:27:04,048
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, eating the fruit, yeah, you can say that you wouldn't be there without a field, but it's certainly not the soil and the field, because you're eating only the thing.

412
00:27:05,689 --> 00:27:09,049
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't be there without a field, but it's clearly not the field.

413
00:27:10,770 --> 00:27:12,570
[SPEAKER_00]: How long does it take to become virtuous?

414
00:27:14,182 --> 00:27:14,962
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as it's necessary.

415
00:27:17,583 --> 00:27:21,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, some people can do it, you know, in a half a night.

416
00:27:21,084 --> 00:27:25,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people, like, again, some people can do it just after speaking with Buddha for a while.

417
00:27:25,344 --> 00:27:28,845
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind will be virtuous enough for them to get the right view.

418
00:27:29,685 --> 00:27:38,547
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's because they don't have the right instruction without any kind of frequency notions obstructing it.

419
00:27:39,448 --> 00:27:40,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, virtue is not just restraint?

420
00:27:42,154 --> 00:27:47,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the restraint is supposed to bring the state of mind.

421
00:27:48,216 --> 00:27:49,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the virtue state of mind.

422
00:27:50,016 --> 00:27:53,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue is a network like behavior and attitude.

423
00:27:55,157 --> 00:27:56,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a recognition.

424
00:27:56,938 --> 00:27:57,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's...

425
00:27:58,038 --> 00:27:59,839
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been engaging in virtuous behavior.

426
00:28:01,039 --> 00:28:01,839
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a recognition.

427
00:28:01,939 --> 00:28:02,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So it does take time.

428
00:28:02,579 --> 00:28:05,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can get, yeah, to, as I said,

429
00:28:06,160 --> 00:28:10,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people can get established upon that recognition quickly, upon one virtuous act.

430
00:28:11,305 --> 00:28:12,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people need more.

431
00:28:13,066 --> 00:28:14,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Today, I think, most people need much more.

432
00:28:15,669 --> 00:28:16,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Much more time.

433
00:28:16,750 --> 00:28:23,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, much more of virtuous acts in order to get the recognition of a phenomenon of virtue which you're established upon.

434
00:28:26,599 --> 00:28:29,261
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you do practice dhamma, that goes in a completely different direction.

435
00:28:30,022 --> 00:28:31,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you weren't, means you could do

436
00:28:32,867 --> 00:28:44,116
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma means there would be set of prescribed rites, rituals, duties, methods, techniques, vipassana retreats that could lead you to the dhamma.

437
00:28:46,478 --> 00:28:51,282
[SPEAKER_01]: A mere performance, mechanical performance of something, could lead you to the dhamma, but it can't.

438
00:28:53,624 --> 00:28:54,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Because dhamma doesn't pretend to that.

439
00:29:01,672 --> 00:29:10,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why does virtue... why does virtue have to be the base?

440
00:29:13,179 --> 00:29:17,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that question relevant?

441
00:29:26,148 --> 00:29:26,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, I can answer it.

442
00:29:27,789 --> 00:29:29,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Back onto the simile of going out on a motorway,

443
00:29:30,906 --> 00:29:39,309
[SPEAKER_01]: In pursuing your senses, in exposing yourself to accidents or a chopping block or whatever else, say no to it by default.

444
00:29:40,050 --> 00:29:41,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it better than saying yes?

445
00:29:43,251 --> 00:29:44,711
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a danger involved in it?

446
00:29:46,692 --> 00:29:46,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

447
00:29:47,392 --> 00:29:48,413
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's better to say no.

448
00:29:48,793 --> 00:29:49,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better to restrain.

449
00:29:50,253 --> 00:29:51,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why virtue is better.

450
00:29:53,034 --> 00:29:56,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know why, but through doing it, it prevents.

451
00:29:59,053 --> 00:30:01,194
[SPEAKER_01]: It prevents the more immediate demise.

452
00:30:03,334 --> 00:30:07,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know there's trouble in that regard you get, if you don't restrain yourself.

453
00:30:07,975 --> 00:30:08,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

454
00:30:08,255 --> 00:30:09,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that could happen.

455
00:30:09,096 --> 00:30:09,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

456
00:30:09,656 --> 00:30:13,517
[SPEAKER_01]: But it goes... it doesn't just concern the bodily behavior.

457
00:30:13,537 --> 00:30:14,857
[SPEAKER_01]: It goes down to the mind.

458
00:30:15,537 --> 00:30:18,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It works same for the mind, just subtler, but it's the same principle.

459
00:30:18,838 --> 00:30:23,379
[SPEAKER_01]: By letting mind go onto the domain that doesn't own, you're taken by it.

460
00:30:24,099 --> 00:30:26,800
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying no, means you remain out of it.

461
00:30:27,107 --> 00:30:29,307
[SPEAKER_01]: By remaining out of it means you can see it eventually.

462
00:30:30,388 --> 00:30:31,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Not if you're engaged.

463
00:30:32,068 --> 00:30:40,689
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha meant when he said there is no... it's impossible to engage in a sensual act without having your mind being taken by those sensual perceptions.

464
00:30:41,049 --> 00:30:41,529
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

465
00:30:41,930 --> 00:30:44,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm free from sensuality, yet I'm doing sensual acts.

466
00:30:45,890 --> 00:30:47,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on reality, it's impossible.

467
00:30:47,570 --> 00:30:49,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It just cannot... cannot occur.

468
00:30:52,011 --> 00:30:52,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why.

469
00:30:54,432 --> 00:30:56,612
[SPEAKER_01]: A person who is not virtuous, then,

470
00:30:57,200 --> 00:30:59,767
[SPEAKER_01]: It means their minds are assumed in those domains.

471
00:30:59,787 --> 00:31:01,972
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't get a perspective on it, if he is in it.

472
00:31:38,385 --> 00:31:56,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when your general behavior is not engaging in things that are compromising the perspective on your general behavior, you can start seeing that the, well, simultaneous kind of

473
00:31:57,473 --> 00:32:03,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by restraining first, in order to get a perspective, you get a perspective.

474
00:32:27,156 --> 00:32:29,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you see the place of restraint.

475
00:32:30,038 --> 00:32:37,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the place of duty, which means you don't hold on to it blindly anymore, which means you're free from sīla-vād-vādanāsa.

476
00:32:39,564 --> 00:32:41,425
[SPEAKER_01]: And, anyway, you don't have to restrain that.

477
00:32:41,926 --> 00:32:44,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will, because that's now the established normal behavior.

478
00:32:46,069 --> 00:32:54,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, because you recognize it's more less hassle and more pleasant to be restrained.

479
00:32:55,135 --> 00:32:59,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the joy of renunciation replaces the joy of sensual indulgence.

480
00:32:59,677 --> 00:33:02,318
[SPEAKER_01]: But to get any further, you have to...

481
00:33:02,338 --> 00:33:03,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It is our view for that.

482
00:33:04,819 --> 00:33:06,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just do pure restraint.

483
00:33:06,760 --> 00:33:10,561
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can be telling yourself, but it won't take root, because it's not enough.

484
00:33:16,084 --> 00:33:24,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you get that general perspective, and you see that whatever choice you make, made, will make, your general experience, your experience as a whole,

485
00:33:25,269 --> 00:33:32,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fully dependent upon this random physical body right here, right now, that's the most close to you.

486
00:33:32,953 --> 00:33:36,375
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, there's this body, there are these bodies.

487
00:33:36,435 --> 00:33:37,636
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no difference between them.

488
00:33:37,996 --> 00:33:40,157
[SPEAKER_01]: They are bodies.

489
00:33:40,218 --> 00:33:43,159
[SPEAKER_01]: In those bodies, they're internally, externally, internally and externally.

490
00:33:43,219 --> 00:33:43,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Bodies are there.

491
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:51,404
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that this particular body is closer to the general experience as a whole, determines it more than these bodies.

492
00:33:51,424 --> 00:33:53,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of it can be mine to begin with.

493
00:33:54,429 --> 00:33:57,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, getting that kind of perspective means good or bad.

494
00:33:57,710 --> 00:33:58,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Good and bad.

495
00:33:58,691 --> 00:33:59,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

496
00:34:00,051 --> 00:34:00,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Both.

497
00:34:00,592 --> 00:34:01,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

498
00:34:01,612 --> 00:34:03,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impermanent, because it's within that.

499
00:34:03,613 --> 00:34:04,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot escape.

500
00:34:04,333 --> 00:34:05,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot spill over.

501
00:34:05,174 --> 00:34:06,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot step outside.

502
00:34:10,096 --> 00:34:11,697
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's completely different than just doing good.

503
00:34:11,757 --> 00:34:12,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It's absolutely nothing.

504
00:34:12,717 --> 00:34:16,599
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon good, inasmuch you abandon bad.

505
00:34:21,301 --> 00:34:24,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to do so, you need to be established upon good.

506
00:34:25,332 --> 00:34:26,393
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is it good?

507
00:34:26,773 --> 00:34:41,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Because at least you don't know why, but by doing good, not bad, means at least you kind of don't step into that domain where you fully lose control, fully lose perspective, fully take it back.

508
00:34:41,764 --> 00:34:43,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't take the bait, basically.

509
00:34:43,346 --> 00:34:44,066
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's good.

510
00:34:44,667 --> 00:34:50,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it says no to... you don't even know that it's bait, but by adopting an attitude of saying no to it, you don't take it.

511
00:34:50,911 --> 00:34:53,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And later on, you find out that there is actually poison in it.

512
00:34:53,833 --> 00:34:54,494
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a simile

513
00:34:55,193 --> 00:35:05,396
[SPEAKER_01]: I just wish you'd understand that the poisonous drink, in terms of the sensual pleasure, pleasant, pleasing in every sense, sight, smell, texture, everything.

514
00:35:05,756 --> 00:35:08,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know now that there is a poison to it.

515
00:35:08,096 --> 00:35:09,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in the beginning, why am I saying no to this?

516
00:35:09,997 --> 00:35:10,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just pleasure.

517
00:35:10,757 --> 00:35:11,317
[SPEAKER_01]: It's innocent.

518
00:35:11,357 --> 00:35:11,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nice.

519
00:35:11,917 --> 00:35:12,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beautiful.

520
00:35:12,577 --> 00:35:13,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever else.

521
00:35:13,578 --> 00:35:14,418
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole world does it.

522
00:35:14,518 --> 00:35:15,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I have to say no?

523
00:35:16,778 --> 00:35:17,399
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know that.

524
00:35:17,419 --> 00:35:19,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you would, you would be free from it.

525
00:35:20,259 --> 00:35:21,640
[SPEAKER_01]: But you take it and trust it.

526
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:22,760
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better to say no.

527
00:35:23,988 --> 00:35:25,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And you ask, why is it better?

528
00:35:25,809 --> 00:35:30,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And I say, because you don't consume it.

529
00:35:30,931 --> 00:35:33,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Just... you don't know why it's better not to consume it.

530
00:35:34,072 --> 00:35:37,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Later on, when you find out, you can find out only because you haven't been consuming it.

531
00:35:37,834 --> 00:35:39,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's effortless to restrain.

532
00:35:40,255 --> 00:35:40,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Afterwards, yes.

533
00:35:42,276 --> 00:35:51,339
[SPEAKER_01]: After there is no restraint, it's understanding, which is why I cannot engage in a sexual intercourse or any sensual act.

534
00:35:51,460 --> 00:35:52,560
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

535
00:35:53,968 --> 00:35:57,951
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not restraining himself, really, kind of trying hard enough to do it.

536
00:35:57,991 --> 00:35:59,512
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't even conceive to do it.

537
00:36:05,898 --> 00:36:08,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's better not to do it in the beginning.

538
00:36:09,020 --> 00:36:10,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, afterwards, it becomes self-evident.

539
00:36:12,383 --> 00:36:20,289
[SPEAKER_01]: The sotāpanna sees that, though he still is... Yeah, he sees that in general, on a fundamental level, but more particularly, he still has worries.

540
00:36:20,309 --> 00:36:21,990
[SPEAKER_01]: He still has... Yeah.

541
00:36:24,621 --> 00:36:26,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can fall back into those things.

542
00:36:27,423 --> 00:36:28,164
[SPEAKER_01]: To an extent.

543
00:36:28,464 --> 00:36:35,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't fall back to the point of having a wrong view of Atharvāda anymore.

544
00:36:35,650 --> 00:36:36,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

545
00:36:37,851 --> 00:36:40,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, at most, seven rebirths.

546
00:36:40,053 --> 00:36:42,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But remember that sutta, sometimes it's the second one, that's it.

547
00:36:43,136 --> 00:36:44,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the third one, that's it.

548
00:36:44,437 --> 00:36:46,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Seven at most, but not necessarily all seven.

549
00:36:48,500 --> 00:36:52,343
[SPEAKER_01]: One becomes, upon landing, in all those different turns slightly,

550
00:36:53,405 --> 00:36:55,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Slightly ambiguous descriptions.

551
00:36:56,206 --> 00:36:57,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't even get fully born as human.

552
00:36:57,847 --> 00:37:04,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Just upon landing, to be reborn, whatever that means, he becomes, he becomes an anant.

553
00:37:11,231 --> 00:37:14,412
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the parents reborn doesn't need much to remember again?

554
00:37:15,693 --> 00:37:21,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he might, he might not, like, he doesn't remember, he might not remember particular things,

555
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:26,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But the principles that pertain to the nature of memory are there.

556
00:37:26,923 --> 00:37:28,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And he just needs to be alive.

557
00:37:29,485 --> 00:37:38,131
[SPEAKER_01]: And if he's not, well, in the next life, then the next life, at most seven lifetimes, and those principles will kind of seep back into whatever understanding he has.

558
00:37:41,673 --> 00:37:44,635
[SPEAKER_02]: Probably he's very naturally inclined towards the world's teaching.

559
00:37:44,655 --> 00:37:48,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, naturally inclined means, basically, those principles are what he is, so...

560
00:37:49,495 --> 00:37:52,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it will, inevitably, because that's the direction here.

561
00:37:52,658 --> 00:37:56,721
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether he wants to go this way, he'll be going that way, eventually.

562
00:37:58,183 --> 00:38:05,929
[SPEAKER_01]: And no matter how indulgent he can get, and careless, it's all, like, just nothing in comparison.

563
00:38:06,230 --> 00:38:07,210
[SPEAKER_01]: He will arrive there.

564
00:38:08,151 --> 00:38:14,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, there is still some work to be done, so there's no excuse for not doing it, which is why the body would be urgent.

565
00:38:15,891 --> 00:38:22,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, I've been saying, eh...

566
00:38:22,695 --> 00:38:38,785
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to gain the perspective, eh... that this general awareness is... depends on this... these particulars, body.

567
00:38:41,466 --> 00:38:43,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And then doing it, and then seeing that

568
00:38:46,611 --> 00:38:51,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're not entirely free from suffering, but there's a major shift.

569
00:38:51,553 --> 00:38:53,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You're no longer subject to it.

570
00:38:54,935 --> 00:38:59,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have no doubts, in regard to what you've been doing.

571
00:39:01,998 --> 00:39:05,759
[SPEAKER_00]: At the same, you know, once it works, so there's the effect of the karma.

572
00:39:06,660 --> 00:39:10,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that's worked.

573
00:39:12,942 --> 00:39:14,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can't even conceive.

574
00:39:15,209 --> 00:39:32,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Any idea of a cameo existing upon anywhere else, different from your general experience as a whole, dependent upon these material organs, which is also part of general experience as a whole, as a form of designation, these organs.

575
00:39:41,549 --> 00:39:43,971
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not to change the

576
00:39:45,009 --> 00:39:51,512
[SPEAKER_00]: In general awareness, how does that happen?

577
00:39:52,152 --> 00:39:54,853
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just an attention to the whole.

578
00:39:55,753 --> 00:39:56,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not happening.

579
00:39:56,573 --> 00:39:57,414
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already there.

580
00:39:59,395 --> 00:40:01,395
[SPEAKER_01]: You step back, you recognize it.

581
00:40:01,855 --> 00:40:07,558
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you can reflect is because there are... the nature of reflection is possible.

582
00:40:09,758 --> 00:40:12,439
[SPEAKER_01]: If there wouldn't be a place...

583
00:40:12,459 --> 00:40:12,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

584
00:40:15,407 --> 00:40:16,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something I'm doing.

585
00:40:16,588 --> 00:40:23,012
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something... Well, you do it by intending to go that direction, but that direction exists.

586
00:40:23,192 --> 00:40:26,134
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what your experience is.

587
00:40:27,835 --> 00:40:36,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, general and particular, actual and peripheral, peripheral being general, that's already there.

588
00:40:37,181 --> 00:40:42,885
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you're so used to being absorbed in what's taking your attention actually, right now, directly.

589
00:40:43,577 --> 00:40:48,461
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see peripheral, then you wonder how this peripheral comes... No, that peripheral is the basis for actual.

590
00:40:49,702 --> 00:40:53,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the same sense, that peripheral is determined by that which is not peripheral.

591
00:40:54,526 --> 00:40:55,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, it's peripheral to this.

592
00:40:58,209 --> 00:41:01,772
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing the phenomenon of that peripheral means developing mindfulness.

593
00:41:04,515 --> 00:41:07,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Like learning how to see without shifting your vision directly.

594
00:41:08,558 --> 00:41:09,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to see peripherally.

595
00:41:11,243 --> 00:41:13,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I'm trying to do it, it doesn't really work, isn't it?

596
00:41:13,924 --> 00:41:24,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yeah, if you try to do it, if you intend it, or rather over-intend it, in this case, means you just shift onto a new actual, which means now peripheral is different, and you're not seeing it.

597
00:41:25,706 --> 00:41:35,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see it in regard to what you're seeing, which is a form of seeing, an indirect way of seeing, which is an equally valid way of seeing.

598
00:41:36,049 --> 00:41:37,109
[SPEAKER_01]: You just got so used to

599
00:41:39,964 --> 00:41:42,471
[SPEAKER_01]: The norm of existence and reality.

600
00:41:43,313 --> 00:41:45,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if something is not there, well, it doesn't exist.

601
00:41:45,860 --> 00:41:46,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unreal.

602
00:42:08,222 --> 00:42:11,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And general awareness is that the... what is another term for it?

603
00:42:13,225 --> 00:42:13,705
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

604
00:42:14,746 --> 00:42:21,570
[SPEAKER_00]: I say, reflect... Mindfulness.

605
00:42:21,590 --> 00:42:22,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is present.

606
00:42:23,671 --> 00:42:24,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's conscious.

607
00:42:24,872 --> 00:42:25,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

608
00:42:27,073 --> 00:42:28,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is established.

609
00:42:36,015 --> 00:42:40,757
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't establish it by going somewhere deep inside of your head or whatever else.

610
00:42:41,477 --> 00:42:43,958
[SPEAKER_01]: You establish it by recognizing your general experience.

611
00:42:43,998 --> 00:42:52,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now, you speak fully determined by this positioned matter of your body on that chair, while we're speaking, while you're asking these questions.

612
00:42:52,982 --> 00:42:57,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That body remains there, seated, for whichever, whichever situation it's in.

613
00:42:58,604 --> 00:43:01,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no control over the material aspect of that.

614
00:43:03,684 --> 00:43:06,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you have no control to such an extent that you can't even think matter.

615
00:43:07,525 --> 00:43:10,507
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think in regard to that which matter is.

616
00:43:10,527 --> 00:43:15,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And even that which you think that matter is, in regard to which you're thinking, that's also a thought.

617
00:43:15,989 --> 00:43:19,070
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how inaccessible elements are.

618
00:43:21,311 --> 00:43:24,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And consciousness is out there with them, equally inaccessible.

619
00:43:24,732 --> 00:43:32,715
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will realize that whatever comes as a result of conscious matter, namely feeling, perception, intentions, are equally inaccessible.

620
00:43:34,062 --> 00:43:41,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my sense of myself, me, volition, intention, determination are not mine.

621
00:43:41,888 --> 00:43:43,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable to be mine.

622
00:43:44,350 --> 00:43:46,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is... remember we spoke about it a few days ago.

623
00:43:47,713 --> 00:43:57,021
[SPEAKER_01]: We said when it's so fundamentally inconceivable, death is like... it's absolutely nothing to grieve over.

624
00:43:57,101 --> 00:44:02,005
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to even conceive a notion of grief, sadness over it.

625
00:44:02,926 --> 00:44:18,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, in that Udana, the Buddha said, there is the unborn, the undetermined, all of those, like there is, and that's that inaccessible thing.

626
00:44:19,057 --> 00:44:22,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if there wouldn't be, nibbāna is the inaccessible.

627
00:44:22,559 --> 00:44:23,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real.

628
00:44:23,540 --> 00:44:29,884
[SPEAKER_01]: In every experience, in the experience of a Buddhist, non-Buddhist, patujana, arahant, nibbāna is there.

629
00:44:30,145 --> 00:44:31,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just whether it's fully understood or not.

630
00:44:34,748 --> 00:44:37,689
[SPEAKER_01]: If there wouldn't be inaccessible, means access would be there.

631
00:44:39,069 --> 00:44:40,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And freedom from it would not be possible.

632
00:44:41,490 --> 00:44:46,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But because there is inaccessible, freedom from anything that you could have accessed before, is possible.

633
00:44:48,091 --> 00:44:51,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And accessible is anything that appears.

634
00:44:51,652 --> 00:44:54,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you can access it, you can take it, you can appropriate it.

635
00:44:56,413 --> 00:45:00,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, birth, aging, death, and so on, as a result of it.

636
00:45:02,214 --> 00:45:04,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you fully understand that it's rooted

637
00:45:06,062 --> 00:45:07,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Upon something you can't even think.

638
00:45:09,523 --> 00:45:15,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that, means you know what inaccessible is, without conceiving it in your thoughts.

639
00:45:22,528 --> 00:45:25,109
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely, like, no room for emotion.

640
00:45:25,129 --> 00:45:27,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

641
00:45:30,271 --> 00:45:31,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why one doesn't feel anything.

642
00:45:36,322 --> 00:45:43,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, as well, you can resolve upon that, with wisdom, long enough to become an arahant.

643
00:45:44,007 --> 00:45:51,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you keep that image long enough, correctly, without conceiving it, all of the factors will fall away.

644
00:45:52,632 --> 00:45:54,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what the seeker is supposed to do.

645
00:45:55,354 --> 00:45:56,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Not me, not mine, not I am.

646
00:45:57,575 --> 00:46:01,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Not existing here, not existing there, not assuming I exist like this, not assuming I exist like that.

647
00:46:03,379 --> 00:46:05,420
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the element of nibbāna,

648
00:46:06,068 --> 00:46:07,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Like nibbāna dāt, that's what it means.

649
00:46:08,469 --> 00:46:13,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there as a possibility, as an element, but it needs to be understood.

650
00:46:13,991 --> 00:46:14,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, to get established.

651
00:46:15,351 --> 00:46:16,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it needs to be understood.

652
00:46:16,372 --> 00:46:18,773
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... and that's why you can conceive it as well.

653
00:46:19,533 --> 00:46:31,957
[SPEAKER_01]: In that other sutta, it says, he thinks, oh, I am in nibbāna, I am entering nibbāna, and that, which he refers to, is actually nibbāna, but he's not entering it or attaining it, because he's thinking, I am.

654
00:46:32,918 --> 00:46:33,718
[SPEAKER_01]: He's conceiving it.

655
00:46:38,078 --> 00:46:39,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's nothing magical.

656
00:46:39,218 --> 00:46:41,319
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing hidden, in that sense.

657
00:46:41,399 --> 00:46:44,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what you have is patuji's five aggregates.

658
00:46:44,861 --> 00:46:46,161
[SPEAKER_01]: What shows that it's five aggregates.

659
00:46:48,902 --> 00:46:51,083
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within that, that the whole dhamma is contained.

660
00:46:51,884 --> 00:46:54,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, it pertains to the way you take them, or not.

661
00:46:55,525 --> 00:46:56,305
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates.

662
00:46:59,126 --> 00:47:07,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And you take them, for as long as you don't fully understand that, eh... that it is impossible to take them in the first place.

663
00:47:08,925 --> 00:47:09,906
[SPEAKER_01]: They're inaccessible.

664
00:47:11,547 --> 00:47:16,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that they're inaccessible, you access them, through that lack of knowledge.

665
00:47:16,790 --> 00:47:20,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no room for it, but it has always been there.

666
00:47:20,672 --> 00:47:24,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's beginningless, but actually, it's not supposed to be there.

667
00:47:24,735 --> 00:47:25,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not needed.

668
00:47:25,515 --> 00:47:26,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessary.

669
00:47:26,216 --> 00:47:27,917
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a parasite, as they refer to it.

670
00:47:28,677 --> 00:47:29,478
[SPEAKER_01]: But beginningless.

671
00:47:31,439 --> 00:47:36,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something you knew before, because if you knew before, you couldn't have fallen onto this not knowing.

672
00:47:37,120 --> 00:47:39,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Because once you know, you cannot not know anymore.

673
00:47:41,061 --> 00:47:44,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this lack of knowledge is beginningless, yet completely gratuitous.

674
00:47:47,163 --> 00:47:48,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that they're inaccessible.

675
00:47:49,764 --> 00:47:50,144
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

676
00:48:11,463 --> 00:48:18,406
[SPEAKER_00]: With general mindfulness, we can begin to recognize that khandhas are inaccessible.

677
00:48:18,986 --> 00:48:39,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you recognize that any thought, any notion, any talking, any designation, no matter how subtle, how immoral, how general, how ambiguous, you recognize it as such, and by recognizing it as such, you don't take it to stand, you don't take it to access that which is inaccessible.

678
00:48:40,037 --> 00:48:43,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is back onto the principle of meditation I was telling you about before.

679
00:48:43,580 --> 00:48:44,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever arises, you include it.

680
00:48:47,163 --> 00:48:48,544
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize it as such.

681
00:48:49,445 --> 00:48:50,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

682
00:48:50,406 --> 00:49:03,357
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say, ah, the matter is inaccessible, that thought of that inaccessible matter is also your thought, which you then think that matter to be, so that matter has access to you, or you access the matter.

683
00:49:03,437 --> 00:49:06,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So he conceives in matter, matter in me, I am in matter, I am left, I am right.

684
00:49:07,180 --> 00:49:08,141
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives matter.

685
00:49:10,327 --> 00:49:22,176
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know that matter is inaccessible, and then that thought doesn't stand for that which matter is, and that thought that says that which matter is also doesn't stand for that which matter is, and there's no end to it.

686
00:49:23,417 --> 00:49:37,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you leave the whole infinite kind of domain as a... well, sorry, you don't leave it, but you kind of understand that whole infinite domain as basically inaccessible.

687
00:49:38,088 --> 00:49:40,130
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot access matter, matter cannot access it.

688
00:49:41,360 --> 00:49:50,763
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how far you go, what next thought comes, it cannot stand for that which matter is, because that's another thought that can stand for what matter is.

689
00:49:50,783 --> 00:49:57,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, whatever I think, quickly or slowly, ambiguously, it will be on the level of thought.

690
00:49:57,465 --> 00:50:06,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, that which matter is cannot manifest itself in my soul, because what I think are my thoughts, which is why what I perceive and my perception, what I feel are my feelings.

691
00:50:06,707 --> 00:50:07,627
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't feel my thoughts.

692
00:50:08,308 --> 00:50:09,648
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't perceive my feelings.

693
00:50:11,145 --> 00:50:14,187
[SPEAKER_01]: That confusion means confusion in regard to the basic nature of phenomena.

694
00:50:16,248 --> 00:50:21,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it needs to be clarified to that point, when what I'm saying now can make more sense.

695
00:50:22,712 --> 00:50:27,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you think matter, you direct your mind to matter, that's a thought.

696
00:50:27,595 --> 00:50:28,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize it.

697
00:50:28,695 --> 00:50:32,798
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how far it goes, you recognize it within the domain of thought.

698
00:50:32,838 --> 00:50:33,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Next thought,

699
00:50:33,922 --> 00:50:35,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Still shares the same nature of thought.

700
00:50:36,304 --> 00:50:43,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And you get to understand that when you start concerning yourself with nature of things, as opposed to with particularity of things.

701
00:50:43,349 --> 00:50:45,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't care what you think, you care thought is there.

702
00:50:46,171 --> 00:50:48,293
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't care what you see, you care sight is there.

703
00:50:49,154 --> 00:51:02,524
[SPEAKER_01]: You start recognizing the nature of things by being established in a nature of thought, nature of the thought domain, which is basically nature of everything, means whatever you think will be seen

704
00:51:03,011 --> 00:51:09,134
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, which means, it will be seen as rising, ceasing, and persisting while changing, for as long as it's persisting.

705
00:51:09,755 --> 00:51:17,478
[SPEAKER_01]: But it will not be confused, it will not be assumed to stand for that which is outside of the domain of thought.

706
00:51:17,859 --> 00:51:23,341
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the thought outside of the domain of thought cannot actually be outside of the domain of thought, because it's also another thought.

707
00:51:24,062 --> 00:51:25,863
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can't step outside of it.

708
00:51:26,643 --> 00:51:27,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

709
00:51:27,784 --> 00:51:28,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow.

710
00:51:28,704 --> 00:51:32,146
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll be mindful of that nature.

711
00:51:33,147 --> 00:51:33,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Thought.

712
00:51:35,468 --> 00:51:38,489
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to recognize, you have to at the same time see this.

713
00:51:38,569 --> 00:51:38,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

714
00:51:39,050 --> 00:51:39,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It arises on its own.

715
00:51:40,250 --> 00:51:41,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't create that thought.

716
00:51:42,291 --> 00:51:47,714
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recognize it to the extent which has arisen, you're not adding anything to it.

717
00:51:47,894 --> 00:51:48,934
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no assumption to it.

718
00:51:48,954 --> 00:51:50,435
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not taking anything away from it.

719
00:51:51,635 --> 00:52:01,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, by contemplating Sotāpanna, or even the Bodhisattva, previous Buddha Vipassana, before he became the Buddha,

720
00:52:02,188 --> 00:52:13,296
[SPEAKER_01]: He recognized that, and then all he'd done was contemplating arising, manifesting, arising, appearance, and disappearance, persisting or changing in regard to five aggregates.

721
00:52:14,136 --> 00:52:19,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Things in there arising, things manifesting in there arising, things manifesting in there ceasing.

722
00:52:21,322 --> 00:52:21,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

723
00:52:22,883 --> 00:52:25,585
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd done that long enough, he became samsambuddha.

724
00:52:26,285 --> 00:52:28,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same principle works for anybody who wants to become an arahant.

725
00:52:34,123 --> 00:52:35,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Because things do arise on their own.

726
00:52:36,243 --> 00:52:45,625
[SPEAKER_01]: If they weren't, it means you would be their master.

727
00:52:46,866 --> 00:52:51,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... if I recognize feeling, I say that's just a thought.

728
00:52:52,767 --> 00:52:54,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, feeling is mental.

729
00:52:55,207 --> 00:52:58,628
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say that's just a thought, as opposed to what else?

730
00:52:59,748 --> 00:53:01,029
[SPEAKER_01]: What else is not just a thought?

731
00:53:01,369 --> 00:53:02,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Even just a thought is just a thought.

732
00:53:04,502 --> 00:53:05,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I like.

733
00:53:05,843 --> 00:53:06,383
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a thought.

734
00:53:06,723 --> 00:53:08,004
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... well, everything is a thought, then.

735
00:53:08,024 --> 00:53:08,464
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a thought.

736
00:53:08,984 --> 00:53:11,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of it's just a thought, no, that's a thought.

737
00:53:12,145 --> 00:53:14,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, mastering your thoughts, you'll be an arahant.

738
00:53:15,187 --> 00:53:16,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And, funnily enough, there is a sutra that says that.

739
00:53:17,948 --> 00:53:21,589
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't master your abstract scholarly proliferation thoughts.

740
00:53:21,690 --> 00:53:23,730
[SPEAKER_01]: You master the phenomenon of thoughts.

741
00:53:27,592 --> 00:53:29,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything is mental in that sense.

742
00:53:29,673 --> 00:53:31,774
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to discern anything if it

743
00:53:32,523 --> 00:53:39,025
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't, kind of, pertain to your mind, it might come from different directions, but that's where you know it.

744
00:53:40,285 --> 00:53:41,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You just couldn't.

745
00:53:41,826 --> 00:53:42,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling is mental.

746
00:53:43,966 --> 00:53:49,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you say, whatever arises is... Well, mental means it's phenomenon.

747
00:53:50,728 --> 00:53:51,708
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a phenomenon.

748
00:53:53,769 --> 00:53:54,589
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what mind is.

749
00:53:55,269 --> 00:53:57,130
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of the phenomenon is what citta is.

750
00:53:58,590 --> 00:54:00,471
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha asked, well, what are those dhammas?

751
00:54:02,349 --> 00:54:03,009
[SPEAKER_01]: What else can it be?

752
00:54:03,990 --> 00:54:04,850
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the sankhāras?

753
00:54:04,990 --> 00:54:05,831
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the five aggregates.

754
00:54:06,271 --> 00:54:07,052
[SPEAKER_01]: What else can it be?

755
00:54:22,920 --> 00:54:27,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you use thought to describe your discursive thinking,

756
00:54:28,050 --> 00:54:28,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Proliferation.

757
00:54:29,131 --> 00:54:31,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you shouldn't use the same word to describe this.

758
00:54:31,353 --> 00:54:33,574
[SPEAKER_01]: You could refer to it as a phenomenon or a mental image.

759
00:54:34,155 --> 00:54:35,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything is a mental image.

760
00:54:36,717 --> 00:54:38,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be able to discern it as anything.

761
00:54:39,579 --> 00:54:40,660
[SPEAKER_01]: It would not manifest.

762
00:54:44,903 --> 00:54:49,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mental image of this is just thought, is a mental image.

763
00:54:49,567 --> 00:54:50,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Of this is just a thought.

764
00:54:51,428 --> 00:54:52,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thoughts are mental images.

765
00:54:54,651 --> 00:54:55,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Perceptions are mental images.

766
00:54:55,892 --> 00:54:57,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings are mental images.

767
00:54:58,653 --> 00:54:59,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts are dhammas.

768
00:54:59,793 --> 00:55:00,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings are dhammas.

769
00:55:00,734 --> 00:55:01,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Perceptions are dhammas.

770
00:55:03,856 --> 00:55:04,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts are dhammas.

771
00:55:05,237 --> 00:55:05,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

772
00:55:08,280 --> 00:55:14,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So contemplating arising and falling, you're contemplating arising and falling of mental images.

773
00:55:15,385 --> 00:55:15,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

774
00:55:15,765 --> 00:55:17,847
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience has all its foundations.

775
00:55:19,168 --> 00:55:19,808
[SPEAKER_01]: It has arisen.

776
00:55:20,789 --> 00:55:26,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, arising is already persisting while changing.

777
00:55:28,010 --> 00:55:29,852
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just increasing in its presence.

778
00:55:31,173 --> 00:55:37,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But increase, like fading in, means, ah, it's already changing while it's increasing.

779
00:55:39,200 --> 00:55:49,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the three are arising, persisting, sorry, arising, ceasing, and persisting while changing, fully understood, and so on, what the suttas talk about.

780
00:55:49,970 --> 00:55:52,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like three things in a sequence.

781
00:55:53,761 --> 00:55:59,185
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's certainly not what Bhikkhu Bodhi and many others say, like, arising, persisting, or changing, ceasing.

782
00:55:59,385 --> 00:56:05,129
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like a linear observation of something that fully pertains to time, something you can measure.

783
00:56:05,149 --> 00:56:09,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it started, now it's lasting, now it's gone, and I'm fully out of it.

784
00:56:10,633 --> 00:56:10,833
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

785
00:56:11,434 --> 00:56:13,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising is already persisting or changing.

786
00:56:14,336 --> 00:56:16,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Ceasing is already persisting or changing.

787
00:56:17,278 --> 00:56:20,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just like fading in and fading out while persisting or changing.

788
00:56:20,840 --> 00:56:22,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's on two completely different levels.

789
00:56:23,430 --> 00:56:26,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising and ceasing is on one level, persisting or changing is on another level.

790
00:56:28,675 --> 00:56:35,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that already means you cannot not include yourself in that form of contemplation.

791
00:56:36,221 --> 00:56:42,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your general experience as a whole is arising in a certain way, it's persisting or changing.

792
00:56:42,746 --> 00:56:47,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're aware of that, well, you're aware of you being within that.

793
00:56:47,568 --> 00:56:51,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is arising for me, or to me, or left of me, or right of me.

794
00:56:51,810 --> 00:56:54,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's all persisting or changing, and you're mindful of that.

795
00:56:56,833 --> 00:57:00,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in the same sense, when something is fading away, it's still there.

796
00:57:01,155 --> 00:57:02,395
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a form of appearance.

797
00:57:03,496 --> 00:57:04,096
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

798
00:57:04,476 --> 00:57:07,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't find impermanence at the ending of the thing.

799
00:57:07,357 --> 00:57:08,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just another thing there.

800
00:57:09,402 --> 00:57:13,004
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll find it within the appearance, within the persistence of that thing.

801
00:57:13,124 --> 00:57:14,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll find that's why it's impermanent.

802
00:57:15,366 --> 00:57:17,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it possesses on its own.

803
00:57:25,433 --> 00:57:31,457
[SPEAKER_02]: You just woke up.

804
00:57:38,695 --> 00:58:05,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can practice this whole thing in regard to your own body right here, right now, whichever position it is.

805
00:58:06,127 --> 00:58:08,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you think, oh, my body is seated,

806
00:58:08,623 --> 00:58:10,845
[SPEAKER_01]: You think that stands for that matter which your body is.

807
00:58:12,426 --> 00:58:13,827
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a form of conceiving.

808
00:58:15,388 --> 00:58:17,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to have a thought, because you can't but not think.

809
00:58:18,250 --> 00:58:26,456
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to have a thought that my body is still seated, but then you want to leave that thought to be to the extent that it wants to be.

810
00:58:27,877 --> 00:58:31,300
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you have to recognize it phenomenologically, the way it has arisen.

811
00:58:31,780 --> 00:58:36,584
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do, then it will not be conceiving that because of which that thought is.

812
00:58:37,850 --> 00:58:39,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the four great elements.

813
00:58:41,573 --> 00:58:47,177
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I was writing the term indetermination, that because of which, because if I use the direct term, people would just think, ah, that's it.

814
00:58:47,257 --> 00:58:47,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But no.

815
00:58:48,237 --> 00:58:49,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another thought.

816
00:58:49,999 --> 00:59:01,667
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying that because of which that thought is, standing for that because of which that thought is, means, oh, you just conceived that matter because of which your thought is, including the idea of what that matter is.

817
00:59:03,483 --> 00:59:09,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just think of your body posture, but don't think that to be that element that... because of which your body posture is.

818
00:59:10,088 --> 00:59:13,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, leave, or rather imply, that matter as inaccessible.

819
00:59:14,892 --> 00:59:19,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when your thoughts are, that matter is inaccessible, that is not accessing it either, that thought.

820
00:59:20,396 --> 00:59:22,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you peripherally become more mindful.

821
00:59:24,359 --> 00:59:28,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to think about anything else, really, in terms of collecting all the data and information and

822
00:59:30,557 --> 00:59:32,398
[SPEAKER_01]: mapping it all out, it's it's right here.

823
00:59:32,478 --> 00:59:49,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, remain mindful of the four body postures correctly, without them assuming the four great elements, without your material organs standing for that, because of which those material organs are, and you will not access that matter.

824
00:59:51,065 --> 00:59:55,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you'll be you'll be knowing it, so to speak, because you know

825
00:59:56,623 --> 01:00:02,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That this body, this experience as a whole, is determined, then, because of which it's already there.

826
01:00:04,766 --> 01:00:08,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if it weren't determined, it wouldn't have been here, already arisen.

827
01:00:10,628 --> 01:00:14,370
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you stop assuming the existence of those determinations.

828
01:00:17,831 --> 01:00:24,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is another way of saying, you stop having those thoughts then, standing for that because of which your thoughts were in the first place.

829
01:00:25,722 --> 01:00:28,308
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it means, the cessation of all the saṅkharas.

830
01:00:30,333 --> 01:00:32,217
[SPEAKER_01]: The determinations don't exist anymore.

831
01:00:32,458 --> 01:00:35,786
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to determine, because there's nothing to access in the first place.

832
01:01:02,812 --> 01:01:05,954
[SPEAKER_00]: That's interesting.

833
01:01:06,254 --> 01:01:07,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Bodily posture.

834
01:01:08,536 --> 01:01:10,477
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just when I say bodily posture.

835
01:01:12,238 --> 01:01:12,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts.

836
01:01:13,379 --> 01:01:14,360
[SPEAKER_00]: The image of my body.

837
01:01:14,840 --> 01:01:16,581
[SPEAKER_00]: The sight of my body sitting here.

838
01:01:18,123 --> 01:01:19,764
[SPEAKER_01]: What does every posture have in common?

839
01:01:21,645 --> 01:01:24,247
[SPEAKER_01]: What does the image of every posture have in common?

840
01:01:24,267 --> 01:01:25,147
[SPEAKER_01]: The image of the body.

841
01:01:25,508 --> 01:01:25,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

842
01:01:26,651 --> 01:01:32,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why we can just be mindful of the bodies there to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

843
01:01:32,754 --> 01:01:35,335
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, posture in itself is generally enough.

844
01:01:37,395 --> 01:01:39,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, it's not the fundamental.

845
01:01:40,037 --> 01:01:46,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason why it's good to think of posture, because it will keep that mental element, so to speak.

846
01:01:46,379 --> 01:01:53,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it will... it will kind of allow you to not become fully complacent in conceiving

847
01:01:54,258 --> 01:01:56,460
[SPEAKER_01]: The image of your body is there, which your body is.

848
01:01:57,321 --> 01:02:01,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Because posture is sort of a designation.

849
01:02:02,306 --> 01:02:06,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a shape, it's a position of your body, but it's also a designation.

850
01:02:06,890 --> 01:02:17,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Citta, or mental designation, which means, oh, it's always there, because even knowing the body to the extent necessary for the mind, well, that image of the body is already a mental designation.

851
01:02:19,183 --> 01:02:20,983
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's... that was never the problem.

852
01:02:21,003 --> 01:02:27,505
[SPEAKER_01]: You just... Well, the problem was when that designation stood for that because of which that designation was in the first place.

853
01:02:28,085 --> 01:02:31,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Stood for matter, stood for... stood for the five aggregates.

854
01:02:32,686 --> 01:02:36,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you take up five aggregates like the fire takes up its fuel.

855
01:02:39,028 --> 01:02:41,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But they... they don't need you.

856
01:02:41,489 --> 01:02:43,369
[SPEAKER_01]: They completely exist without you anyway.

857
01:02:55,302 --> 01:03:11,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can just be even less particular and be mindful of your bodily posture while you're doing other things and then just regard that image of your bodily posture upon which you're anchored, mindful, as subject to destruction and you can't do anything about it.

858
01:03:13,026 --> 01:03:22,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that image is genuinely subject to destruction and that image is genuinely the determination for your whatever else particular things you're doing while you're mindful of that.

859
01:03:23,210 --> 01:03:29,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Means whatever you're doing, while you're mindful of that which is subject to destruction, you won't fully appropriate.

860
01:03:31,875 --> 01:03:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: You will remain mindful of.

861
01:03:33,796 --> 01:03:34,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

862
01:03:34,036 --> 01:03:44,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I tell you, okay, well, you go walk about in your day, do stuff, but at any... at the next minute, there might be a bullet flying through your brain from some direction.

863
01:03:46,022 --> 01:03:49,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so you will be going about your business eventually.

864
01:03:50,024 --> 01:03:51,645
[SPEAKER_01]: This will always be in the back of your mind.

865
01:03:54,253 --> 01:03:59,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subject to that, because if you were not subject to that, it wouldn't be possible for that even to arise as a possibility.

866
01:04:02,377 --> 01:04:03,117
[SPEAKER_01]: That's contemplation.

867
01:04:03,137 --> 01:04:04,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by... exactly.

868
01:04:04,378 --> 01:04:10,441
[SPEAKER_01]: By contemplating right things, correct things, as impermanent, and suffering, you will abandon everything else.

869
01:04:11,281 --> 01:04:12,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by just applying blame.

870
01:04:12,562 --> 01:04:13,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's all impermanent.

871
01:04:13,462 --> 01:04:14,102
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all changing.

872
01:04:14,122 --> 01:04:15,403
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's all... that's irrelevant.

873
01:04:16,183 --> 01:04:17,484
[SPEAKER_01]: What you want to see as impermanent,

874
01:04:17,915 --> 01:04:21,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean that you see it's destruction, because if you were to see destruction on your body, well, you wouldn't see it.

875
01:04:22,377 --> 01:04:23,118
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be destroyed.

876
01:04:23,598 --> 01:04:29,381
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see it as subject to destruction, which means it's there, it's manifested, it's present.

877
01:04:29,641 --> 01:04:32,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, it's subject to being destroyed.

878
01:04:46,120 --> 01:04:50,304
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing things subject to such-and-such means seeing things in their nature.

879
01:04:54,348 --> 01:05:07,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, be mindful of general experience as a whole, while you're aware of your body right here, right now, in this particular posture, that is, like, fundamental, kind of, pivotal point of your whole general experience.

880
01:05:07,340 --> 01:05:09,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be inconceivable if this body is not alive.

881
01:05:09,602 --> 01:05:10,223
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that simple.

882
01:05:11,438 --> 01:05:16,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're mindful of that alive body in this particular posture, in that particular posture.

883
01:05:16,501 --> 01:05:26,486
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something that on that level, not on the level of your thoughts, whether you think about it or not, it remains subject to be destroyed.

884
01:05:27,707 --> 01:05:29,968
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means you think of it on that level.

885
01:05:30,868 --> 01:05:36,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, whether I think about this or not, even when I forget, it makes no difference to that nature.

886
01:05:36,911 --> 01:05:39,893
[SPEAKER_01]: It remains subjected to destruction.

887
01:05:41,048 --> 01:05:41,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in their domain.

888
01:05:43,050 --> 01:05:47,694
[SPEAKER_01]: And nothing you can do about it.

889
01:05:47,714 --> 01:05:48,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And there will be proper mindfulness.

890
01:05:50,397 --> 01:05:53,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That results in discernment and wisdom.

891
01:05:53,640 --> 01:05:57,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, you know, be here now and hug a tree and feel good about yourself.

892
01:05:58,124 --> 01:05:58,324
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

893
01:05:58,424 --> 01:05:59,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandon everything.

894
01:05:59,305 --> 01:06:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandon the shit before it sinks you with it.

895
01:06:29,459 --> 01:06:42,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if things are happening to you too fast, busy, crowd, you can't, like, follow, well, which you're not supposed to do from the beginning, follow things particularly, but be even more aware of what's happening particularly.

896
01:06:43,505 --> 01:06:47,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You still know the body is there, and that's the reason why all these things can happen in the first place.

897
01:06:47,627 --> 01:06:56,190
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is there, which is subject to destruction and peril, which means, well, whatever is happening right here, right now, is kind of circumstantial.

898
01:06:56,611 --> 01:06:57,411
[SPEAKER_01]: It could have not been.

899
01:06:58,282 --> 01:06:59,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And it certainly will not be.

900
01:07:03,783 --> 01:07:07,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another reason why, like, the whole contemporary person is just so wrong.

901
01:07:08,164 --> 01:07:10,705
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely the opposite direction from what I'm just saying.

902
01:07:11,565 --> 01:07:27,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of looking at the general image of your body, then you're trying to force this kind of constant, actual, direct perception of body moving, moving, touching, touching, sitting, sitting, drinking, drinking, like, absorb, absorb yourself in the moment as hard as you can.

903
01:07:28,890 --> 01:07:30,191
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's completely the other way.

904
01:07:30,951 --> 01:07:34,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want... Well, you don't bother about being absorbed or not.

905
01:07:35,214 --> 01:07:47,101
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to maintain the background image of a body that determines your absorption or lack of it, that determines it, that body which is subject to destruction.

906
01:07:47,742 --> 01:07:55,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, hence, there's absolutely no method or technique or set of mechanical repetition that can take you in that direction.

907
01:07:55,186 --> 01:07:56,007
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a recognition.

908
01:07:56,687 --> 01:07:57,848
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a formal understanding.

909
01:08:11,968 --> 01:08:13,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so this...

910
01:08:15,449 --> 01:08:18,189
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen the body there.

911
01:08:18,869 --> 01:08:20,550
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning the body there.

912
01:08:20,590 --> 01:08:21,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning the body there.

913
01:08:21,390 --> 01:08:22,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's already there.

914
01:08:23,090 --> 01:08:35,032
[SPEAKER_01]: With a general awareness, but seeing that body there is... General awareness already implies the body there, then you discern the body there, as then, because of which, the general awareness is possible in the first place.

915
01:08:35,432 --> 01:08:37,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But by doing that, I'm including myself there.

916
01:08:37,713 --> 01:08:39,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, that's the thing.

917
01:08:39,998 --> 01:08:49,304
[SPEAKER_01]: By doing that, you leave no... no... no room for yourself to step outside and be excluded.

918
01:08:49,985 --> 01:08:50,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you include everything.

919
01:08:51,025 --> 01:08:54,748
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever yourself... yourself tries to move, it will be within that room.

920
01:09:02,533 --> 01:09:06,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't do that by... by trying to, like, reach the most general thought.

921
01:09:07,243 --> 01:09:10,265
[SPEAKER_01]: They will kind of hold all the other thoughts, because there's more general to that then.

922
01:09:10,845 --> 01:09:25,675
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do that by seeing that any thought, no matter how far it goes, or how particularly absorbed the focus is, is determined by this position body out there, that's subject to destruction.

923
01:09:29,438 --> 01:09:33,721
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why matter and body is the inter... rūpa is the same word.

924
01:09:34,081 --> 01:09:35,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Often, it was interchangeable.

925
01:09:37,022 --> 01:09:39,204
[SPEAKER_01]: You would refer to as matter as the matter of your body.

926
01:09:39,944 --> 01:09:40,865
[SPEAKER_01]: We refer to rūpa.

927
01:09:41,145 --> 01:09:41,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is.

928
01:09:42,206 --> 01:09:42,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

929
01:09:42,787 --> 01:09:43,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Kāyā.

930
01:09:44,268 --> 01:09:46,170
[SPEAKER_01]: But, inconceivable, there's no rūpa.

931
01:09:46,890 --> 01:09:48,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And often that's the rūpa you know.

932
01:09:49,132 --> 01:09:52,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But not that, not the actual rūpa, because the actual rūpa is inaccessible.

933
01:09:53,435 --> 01:09:55,978
[SPEAKER_01]: The four elements, inaccessible.

934
01:09:55,998 --> 01:10:00,201
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the closest proximity, as I was talking, in relation to, like, oh, these four bodies.

935
01:10:01,962 --> 01:10:04,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was often referred to with the same word as you would refer to matter.

936
01:10:23,012 --> 01:10:27,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever, even if you have thoughts of confusion, you can't make it all up.

937
01:10:28,776 --> 01:10:35,581
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think that, while this body is positioned here as such, determining your ability to think that in the first place.

938
01:10:37,382 --> 01:10:39,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And that body is subject to destruction, and you know that.

939
01:10:40,944 --> 01:10:49,831
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, you might have to think about it more, but after a while, it just becomes implicit in that image of that body, which means your mindfulness gets established quicker, more proper, more skilled.

940
01:10:53,593 --> 01:11:01,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about changing what has arisen, it's just recognizing the inherent undermining structure of that.

941
01:11:05,462 --> 01:11:05,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

942
01:11:06,023 --> 01:11:06,943
[SPEAKER_01]: It's undermining.

943
01:11:07,744 --> 01:11:09,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't access it directly.

944
01:11:10,863 --> 01:11:11,904
[SPEAKER_01]: That which undermines you.

945
01:11:12,704 --> 01:11:14,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you would, means you would be undermining it.

946
01:11:15,266 --> 01:11:16,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be undermining you.

947
01:11:17,247 --> 01:11:19,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always below your feet, undermining you.

948
01:11:24,711 --> 01:11:29,133
[SPEAKER_01]: To recognize that, means, well, you're not going to hold to anything that's being undermined.

949
01:11:30,214 --> 01:11:37,478
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you also realize, well, actually, yeah, I was not supposed to hold to this, at all, from the start.

950
01:11:38,479 --> 01:11:39,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was never yours to begin with.

951
01:11:40,060 --> 01:11:40,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

952
01:11:41,108 --> 01:11:43,489
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were yours, it wouldn't have been undermined.

953
01:11:50,253 --> 01:12:01,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And though I'm, I'm always there when I'm turning the body or being mindful of the body, by recognizing the nature of the body as subject to destruction.

954
01:12:01,458 --> 01:12:02,459
[SPEAKER_00]: You include that, yeah.

955
01:12:02,739 --> 01:12:04,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can never include directly.

956
01:12:05,336 --> 01:12:06,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sense of self.

957
01:12:06,677 --> 01:12:08,659
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't, oh, me, I'm subject to this.

958
01:12:08,799 --> 01:12:08,999
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

959
01:12:09,099 --> 01:12:13,062
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that it kind of slides somewhere else.

960
01:12:13,602 --> 01:12:14,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sense of self.

961
01:12:14,403 --> 01:12:16,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, what you're thinking is your sense of self.

962
01:12:16,404 --> 01:12:16,785
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

963
01:12:17,545 --> 01:12:19,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only uproot it indirectly.

964
01:12:19,667 --> 01:12:19,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

965
01:12:20,510 --> 01:12:22,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, there is me in this general experience.

966
01:12:22,411 --> 01:12:31,273
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sitting here, I'm breathing, I'm doing this, I'm doing that, while this seated body remains on the chair, fully subject to destruction.

967
01:12:31,313 --> 01:12:36,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that self that you feel will be experienced as not-self.

968
01:12:36,555 --> 01:12:36,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

969
01:12:37,155 --> 01:12:38,315
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why automatically...

970
01:12:38,355 --> 01:12:46,238
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recognize correctly impermanence and suffering, you don't then do, oh, so this is my not-self.

971
01:12:46,936 --> 01:12:47,116
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

972
01:12:47,196 --> 01:12:51,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, your experience as a whole is automatically the experience of not-self.

973
01:12:52,061 --> 01:12:56,346
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that long enough, you will recognize that it's... that's what not-self is.

974
01:12:57,006 --> 01:13:01,871
[SPEAKER_01]: By recognizing what's really not-self, well, you'll stop holding a view of self.

975
01:13:02,972 --> 01:13:09,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you become satipaṭṭhāna, who is free from ātavāda, which is not recognizing what actually not-self is.

976
01:13:13,376 --> 01:13:19,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as you see the not-self in front of you, it means, ah, you excluded your sense of self, ah, which means that's not not-self.

977
01:13:20,259 --> 01:13:22,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do it, it's just what you said.

978
01:13:22,881 --> 01:13:37,848
[SPEAKER_01]: When you... general experiences or whatever you're doing, I'm sitting here talking, whatever else, while my body, while this material body is seated, completely subject to distraction from any direction, and I absolutely cannot even foresee it happening.

979
01:13:38,128 --> 01:13:39,909
[SPEAKER_01]: I just know that it's subject to it.

980
01:13:40,682 --> 01:13:46,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, whatever I do, while I have that perception established, will be non-self.

981
01:13:47,527 --> 01:13:47,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

982
01:13:49,388 --> 01:14:01,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is exactly... See how hard it is, because it's not that hard, once you recognize it, then you know what to look for, if you have the utterance of another explaining it, sufficiently done.

983
01:14:02,237 --> 01:14:05,599
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can see why nobody can become an arahant and live in a sotāpanna by accident.

984
01:14:06,440 --> 01:14:06,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

985
01:14:07,460 --> 01:14:08,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't open it.

986
01:14:08,260 --> 01:14:10,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, many people can do this.

987
01:14:10,201 --> 01:14:11,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you see this subject.

988
01:14:11,522 --> 01:14:13,002
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do it long enough.

989
01:14:14,203 --> 01:14:16,724
[SPEAKER_01]: You can accidentally do it once or twice or three times.

990
01:14:17,304 --> 01:14:26,828
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't accidentally repeat it thousands of times, which is the number of times most likely required for somebody to recognize that as a principle.

991
01:14:27,989 --> 01:14:32,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the effort, manly effort, manly strength, manly, like, repetition and so on.

992
01:14:33,731 --> 01:14:36,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what that... that's what practice is about.

993
01:14:36,700 --> 01:14:46,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing what you're supposed to discern, and then keep doing it, and keep doing it, and keep doing it, without somebody telling you that you're supposed to do that.

994
01:14:46,924 --> 01:14:49,265
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable to do it thousands of times.

995
01:14:49,285 --> 01:14:49,845
[SPEAKER_01]: You just can't.

996
01:14:51,066 --> 01:14:54,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, you can't stumble upon it by a chance.

997
01:14:55,388 --> 01:14:57,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, that was a fortunate night.

998
01:14:57,368 --> 01:14:59,649
[SPEAKER_01]: I just... it came so upon just like that.

999
01:15:00,130 --> 01:15:00,690
[SPEAKER_01]: It all happened.

1000
01:15:00,730 --> 01:15:01,370
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm so lucky.

1001
01:15:01,850 --> 01:15:02,691
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't work like that.

1002
01:15:03,371 --> 01:15:06,012
[SPEAKER_01]: If it did, it's because you spent the whole night doing this work.

1003
01:15:07,595 --> 01:15:08,215
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know.

1004
01:15:08,356 --> 01:15:08,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1005
01:15:09,056 --> 01:15:09,516
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done it.

1006
01:15:09,776 --> 01:15:12,418
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that very reason, it cannot be taken away.

1007
01:15:13,119 --> 01:15:15,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't come through any external circumstance.

1008
01:15:15,500 --> 01:15:15,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

1009
01:15:16,181 --> 01:15:16,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Not given.

1010
01:15:17,221 --> 01:15:18,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be taken away.

1011
01:15:19,203 --> 01:15:20,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognized as already there.

1012
01:15:22,545 --> 01:15:25,627
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it's already there, it has nothing to do with it.

1013
01:15:26,007 --> 01:15:28,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we refer to it as an ancient path.

1014
01:15:28,169 --> 01:15:31,511
[SPEAKER_01]: They just, like, revealed it again.

1015
01:15:31,551 --> 01:15:32,111
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was there.

1016
01:15:32,792 --> 01:15:36,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it weren't there, the escape from what's there would not be possible.

